=== task: multi easy
title: The queue in level order
prompt: Choose **every** statement that is true about the queue in the level-order loop.
* Each node joins the queue once and leaves it once :: Right. That's why level order takes O(n) time.
* Children join at the back, behind every node already waiting :: Right. That's what keeps the levels in order.
* At the start of each round of the level-by-level loop, the queue holds exactly one whole level :: Right. The round before took the old level and added all of the next one.
- Children join at the front, so they're visited next :: That's a stack's order, which goes deep first. In a queue, new items wait at the back.
- The queue never holds more than 2 nodes :: The queue can hold a whole level: about half the nodes of a perfect tree.
- Nodes come out in order of their values, smallest first :: A queue hands nodes out in the order they joined, whatever their values.
hint: First in, first out: where does a new item wait?
hint: How many nodes can one level hold?
right: Every node goes through the queue once; children wait at the back; and each round starts with exactly one level waiting.
wrong: A queue is first in, first out: children wait behind everything already there, so a whole level comes out before the next. Each node joins once and leaves once, and the queue can hold a whole level.

